    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understood, let's focus on the precision and material science aspects of your woodworking challenge. Micro-splintering, even when employing a slicing motion, can indeed be influenced by the cellular structure of the walnut and the chemical composition of its lignin.\n\nWalnut wood is known for its fine grain and tight growth rings, which can make it both beautiful and challenging to work with. The cellular structure of the wood is composed of long, thin cells that run parallel to the grain. When you apply pressure with a chisel, these cells can be compressed and cause the wood to split or splinter, especially if the chisel edge is not perfectly aligned with the grain or if the angle of the bevel is not optimal.\n\nLignin, the complex organic polymer that binds the wood cells together, can also play a role in how the wood reacts to cutting tools. Lignin is more resistant to cutting than cellulose, the main component of wood fibers, and can cause the fibers to separate if the tool's edge is not sharp enough or if the angle of attack is not ideal.\n\nTo minimize micro-splintering, ensure that your chisels are razor-sharp and that the bevel angle is set to around 30 degrees, as previously mentioned",
